From e06899bd3639f52fa039bc6408f3ce1d8d430724 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 10:40:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08016/11362] [outer-loop] The retention diagnostic never
 reached the artifact, so the A/B could not compute its own effective support

`engine_retention` (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6035) is computed on every refinement call and is the
ONLY record of which round the engine store ended up holding. It died on the outcome
object: neither call site copied it into `induction_attempts`, so nothing downstream
could tell a call where retention CHANGED the stored engine (best_round < rounds_seen,
restored=True) from one where it was a structural no-op -- a single round ran, both arms
store the identical engine, and the two "arms" are literally the same agent.

That distinction is the whole readability of a retention A/B. Without it, EFFECTIVE
support is uncomputable, and a null cannot be told apart from a measurement that never
had an opportunity to detect anything. Two additive read-only `dict(...)` copies, at
BOTH call sites -- the STALL path matters most, because that is the one that fires on
first contact with an unseen game, which is exactly what a held-out measurement consists
of. Nothing branches on the value.

WHAT IT IMMEDIATELY SHOWED. Re-reading the 2026-07-28 held-out cells using the two
fields that DID predate this ship (no claim is made about `best_round`, which is
unrecoverable there -- `engine_retention` is None): across 13/13 cells, "ran a
multi-round refinement" and "the generator's output reached the policy" are PERFECTLY
anti-correlated. Zero cells have both; 11 are multi-round without reaching; 2 reach with
a single round. The mechanism is structural -- a second round runs BECAUSE round 1
failed the held-out verifier, whose threshold is 1.0, i.e. the induced world model must
be PERFECT to be accepted at all. When the later rounds fail too, nothing is accepted, so
nothing reaches the policy; and when round 1 passes, refinement stops at one round and
retention is a definitional no-op. 12 of 17 attempts had NO round accepted, and 9 were
blocked by `world_model_accuracy_below_threshold`.

Retention decides WHICH engine is stored. It cannot help when none is admitted. So the
acceptance threshold, not retention, is the candidate lever -- and the A/B's support
condition had to become a conjunction with `generator_output_reached_policy` rather than
a round-count test, because a changed stored engine that never reaches the policy has no
causal path to a banked level however many rounds it ran.

REBUILDS. The edit drifted 12 registered artifacts through `provenance.code`. All 12 were
rebuilt with their own builders and diffed leaf-by-leaf against the committed versions:
0 leaves added, 0 removed, and ZERO substantive changes anywhere. The only movement is
provenance -- `arc_competition_agent.py` 374719 -> 376234 bytes (exactly this diff) and
the reproducibility checksums that hash it -- plus clocks. `wall_s` in the re-captured
rows roughly doubled because the offline capture ran alongside a live GPU grid; it is not
a claimed metric and no semantic value moved. Four of the twelve record no
`rebuild_command`, so their builders were recovered from `provenance.code`; the missing
recipes are worth filling in, since the lint cannot print what an artifact never stored.

